                "role": "assistant",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "## Why Durable Agent Transcripts Matter\n\nAgents that take multi-step actions, call tools, and make decisions over long horizons produce a record of their work. A durable transcript preserves that record so it survives crashes, restarts, and handoffs. Without it, a failed run leaves no trace of what was attempted, what succeeded, or why a particular choice was made.\n\n## Core Benefits\n\nDurable transcripts support several practical needs:\n\n- **Debugging:** Engineers can replay the exact sequence of prompts, tool calls, and results that led to a failure.\n- **Auditing:** Organizations can verify what an agent did, which matters for compliance and accountability.\n- **Recovery:** A run interrupted midway can resume from its last recorded state rather than starting over and repeating side effects.\n- **Evaluation:** Stored transcripts become datasets for measuring quality and comparing model or prompt changes.\n\nDurability also requires that writes be ordered and complete. A transcript that silently drops the final tool result can mislead both humans and the agent itself on restart. A minimal append-only record makes this concrete:\n\n```typescript\ntype Entry = { step: number; role: \"agent\" | \"tool\"; content: string };\n\nconst log: Entry[] = [];\nfunction append(entry: Entry): void {\n  log.push(Object.freeze({ ...entry }));\n}\n```\n\n## Conclusion\n\nTreating transcripts as durable infrastructure rather than disposable logs turns agent behavior from an opaque process into something inspectable, recoverable, and trustworthy."
                  }
                ],
